+        {
+          "section": "4",
+          "text": "# 4. Extensibility Principles\n\nThe five original principle headings remain. They guide ordinary compatible extensions and separately assessed necessary normative revision under common governing contracts; no new AP identifiers are allocated here.\n\n---\n\n## 4.1. Backward Compatibility\n\nOrdinary compatible extensions shall preserve the applicable meanings and assigned same-document key association of existing identities under their governing contracts. Compatibility requires actual prior/new provider/consumer/representation/reference assessment, not merely unchanged names or an additive label.\n\nA necessary incompatible normative revision must follow §9 with explicit responsible authority, rationale/alternatives, affected exact owners/consumers/interfaces, mappings/history, Version/Revision and transition checks. It is not silently treated as a compatible extension. Preserve allocated/retired reservations, intended historical interpretation and immutable snapshot members; no arbitrary key reassignment or universal guarantee of live reference validity follows.\n\n---\n\n## 4.2. Canonical Preservation\n\nAn ordinary implementation extension shall not silently redefine responsible canonical document-key/domain semantics. Document Identity remains the semantic owner under applicable common Architecture/Policy, while other descriptor/category/connection/registration/projection owners retain their contracts.\n\nThe canonical model is an architectural contract, distinct from mutable support records and runtime representations. Necessary assessed normative/foundational revision follows common evolution principles and §9; core definition is not made unchangeable for all future governed editions merely by calling it canonical.\n\nDID-014§14 still states additive/backward-compatible-only extensions and that core identity semantics are not extensible. Preserve that actual peer restriction and its pending owner/authority reconciliation with the controlled-evolution approach; this source-local correction neither waives it nor claims universal agreement or implementation permission.\n\n---\n\n## 4.3. Non-Destructive Evolution\n\nPrefer additive, compatible evolution where it meets the actual need without competing semantics or unnecessary disruption. Preserve original evidence, assignments/reservations, same-document continuity and historical exact-snapshot interpretation.\n\nAdditive-only is not an absolute ban on a necessary normative/foundational revision under ARC-002 AP-015–018. Such revision requires §9 prior/new rules, responsible authority, alternatives/rationale, exact owner/consumer/required-interface impact, compatibility/version and transition evidence. No extension may silently invalidate governing assignments, rewrite historic failed checks or claim completed consumer propagation without evidence.\n\n---\n\n## 4.4. Separation of Concerns\n\nRespect actual semantic and record owners under common architecture and declared domains. Identity meaning, descriptors, categories, connections, registrations and graph projections have distinct responsibilities. Required consumption, documentary authority, projection, navigation and execution are separately typed; conceptual separation does not remove indispensable requirements.\n\nAn ordinary extension shall not silently transfer ownership or invent new components. A necessary architectural responsibility/interface revision requires explicit prior/new assessed authority, allocation/contract impact, affected sources/consumers and transition evidence under §9; no such transfer is executed here. Governance is an area of four component contracts, not a single new operational owner. Preserve actual reciprocal Metadata/Identity required claims for scope/interface reconciliation; relabelling or projection does not dissolve a genuine required cycle.\n\n---\n\n## 4.5. Technology Independence\n\nThe five original independence subjects remain:\n\n- programming languages;\n- storage technologies;\n- serialization formats;\n- repositories;\n- implementation frameworks.\n\nLogical document identity meaning shall not be silently redefined by those implementation choices. Explicit governed canonical representation, logical schema/interface and integration constraints remain legitimate; independence does not exempt actual adapters/executors from applicable owner/authority/evidence obligations.\n\nActual implementations record exact inputs/outputs, mappings/source editions/context, contributors, checks, observations and limitations. A legitimate domain-preserving adapter is distinct from competing implementation-specific identity semantics. No runtime, installed tool, universal format suitability or complete interoperability is certified.\n\n---\n\n"
