+          "section": "7",
+          "text": "# 7. Extension Constraints\n\nThe seven original DID-E labels remain source-local extension constraints, not normalized document allocations or newly invented global registrations. Apply them to declared subjects/operations under responsible contracts; they do not grant a universal permission or automatic compatibility verdict.\n\n---\n\n## 7.1. DID-E-001\n\nPreserve existing assigned qualified document-key/same-document association and historical interpretation under its governing contract. Canonical labeling alone establishes neither assignment evidence norfull validity of every record/reference. Mandatory field/state/lifecycle authority and coverage remain explicit; no arbitrary reassignment orsilent historical retargeting is permitted.\n\n---\n\n## 7.2. DID-E-002\n\nPreserve existing assigned identifiers and canonical interpretation across ordinary evolution. Current Family/Type/location/format/status/edition changes do not regenerate stable allocation series. Necessary representation/domain/contract transitions require§9 prior/new mapping, authority, owner/consumer, aliases/reservations/collisions/history and intended reference/exact-snapshot continuity; no reuse ofallocated/retired keys for another document.\n\n---\n\n## 7.3. DID-E-003\n\nAssess affected actual architectural relationships and preserve compatible required contracts where feasible. Do not promise that every previous relationship remains valid forever. Typed document endpoints require intended qualified document/exact-edition/context; other endpoint kinds retain their own contracts. Connection records remain with Relationships, registrations with Registry and projections with Knowledge Graph.\n\nIncoming references may be0..N; optional incoming population does not waive validity of each actual reference. Observe dangling, ambiguous, stale or unavailable targets, alias interpretation and current versus historical context. Stable identity does not guarantee perpetual live retrieval orcomplete connection validity. Necessary contract change follows §9 with actual owner/consumer and required-interface evidence; never silently create orretarget akey orimmutable historical member to make acheck pass.\n\n---\n\n## 7.4. DID-E-004\n\nPreserve full qualified Domain+Identifier uniqueness under its declared canonicalization/allocation contract with actual assignment, population, current/historical collision/reservation evidence and coverage. Copies, legitimate aliases, projections, exact editions and equal suffixes across different series do not alone constitute competing allocations. Missing history/index entries orsyntax success prove neither availability norcomplete uniqueness.\n\n---\n\n## 7.5. DID-E-005\n\nPreserve assigned same-document association, allocated/retired reservations and intended historical recognition under responsible contracts. Retirement needs actual decision/scope and history; document Status orfile/edition/index deletion alone does not retire the logical identity orgrant a missing lifecycle edge. Permanence does not freeze support records orpromise perpetual content availability.\n\n---\n\n## 7.6. DID-E-006\n\nPreserve assigned qualified key and same-document association during ordinary evolution. Supporting state/reference/descriptor/registration/projection records may legitimately evolve under their owners. Before necessary governing-contract/representation change require§9 prior/new authority, compatibility, mappings/history/reference/exact-snapshot continuity and later exact validation; no arbitrary key mutation, reassignment orhistorical evidence rewrite is authorized.\n\n---\n\n## 7.7. DID-E-007\n\nAn ordinary extension shall not silently redefine Document Identity's document-key/domain ownership under applicable common Architecture/Policy. Descriptor/category/connection/registration/projection owners retain their meaning and records. A necessary architectural responsibility/interface revision requires explicit prior/new assessed authority, scope, affected exact owners/consumers and transition evidence under §9; no such transfer is performed here.\n\nSemantic ownership is not sole authority overevery rule, operational permission orabsence of actual required dependencies. Preserve reciprocal Metadata/Identity claims and mandatory peer authority visibly; changing a relationship label orcalling data a projection cannot remove an indispensable contract.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "8",
+          "text": "# 8. Relationship with Other Components\n\nAll six original subjects remain with typed component/area and record/contract roles. Actual required edges use consumer→provider under ARC-004/005 with explicit scope, source editions and interfaces. Documentary reference, semantic association, projection, navigation and execution are distinct; no mandatory universal route orcomplete graph is implied.\n\n| Original subject | Kind | Extension responsibility / boundary |\n|---|---|---|\n| Metadata | Component | Descriptor/model/schema/profile/instance owner; Metadata→Document Identity for identity of the applicable normalized document described; actual reciprocal claim below remains |\n| Classification | Component | Category/assignment owner; applicable classified-document key consumption does not allocate DF IDs to every category/entity |\n| Registry | Component | Registration/index owner; intended qualified document/edition/source/freshness evidence; registration is not allocation or a universal intermediary |\n| Relationships | Component | Typed connection/endpoint/provenance owner; document endpoints use identity contracts, other endpoints retain theirs; no silent key creation/retargeting |\n| Knowledge Graph | Component | Logical node/edge projection/properties with authoritative identity/connection source editions/context and refresh limits; no competing semantic ownership |\n| Governance | Area | Workflow, Policies, Reviews, Baselines documentary contracts; actual DMS/tool/manual operation and CMS change scope separate |\n\nThe functional DID README states: “The Document Identity component depends on the Metadata component for the canonical definition of metadata concepts used to describe document identity.” Current DID-009§5.1.1/§10 retains Document Identity→Metadata alongside Metadata→Document Identity. Preserve both actual required claims pending responsible core-versus-descriptor/interface/source-edition reconciliation. Different scope names, projection ornavigation relabelling do not dissolve a genuine required cycle; no complete globally acyclic graph is certified.\n\nGovernance decisions need actual authority/rules, exact subject/edition/context, executor and observations. Baselines requires exact eligible approved-edition selection and relevant pinned relationship context ortraceable verification evidence. Logical identity orlocal PASS does not approve/publish a Draft successor, select its membership orretarget immutable historical snapshots. Mandatory field/state/lifecycle authority remains pending owner work; no requirement is waived by an extension table.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "9",
+          "text": "# 9. Evolution Strategy\n\nThe received five-label view is retained as a typed documentary proposal/evidence/decision view. It is not a universal operational pipeline or automatic status promotion. Original text/digest and section anchor remain external evidence; no historic view identifier is invented.\n\n<!-- original-section-view: 9 -->\n**Previously unnamed view in §9 — Evolution artifacts, checks and decision boundaries.**\n\n| Original label | Artifact / role | Required evidence / limit |\n|---|---|---|\n| Current Specification | Identified actual input contract/edition | Exact rules, bytes, authority, subjects/context and known unresolved peer claims |\n| Architectural Extension | Scoped proposed addition ornecessary assessed revision | Actual need, alternatives, prior/new owner/consumer/interface impact, compatibility and transition proposal; not permission from a label |\n| Compatibility Verification | Actual scoped evaluation evidence | Provider/consumer/representation/reference/edition/context checks, executor, observations, coverage and limits; missing/conflicted authority withholds broader conformity |\n| Constraint Verification | Actual applicable mandatory-rule evidence | Responsible constraints/editions, checks/observations and negative results under DID-007; not an allocation/repair/approval decision |\n| Canonical Specification | Received name for intended governed specification outcome | A resulting working edition remains at its actual declared lifecycle; local checks do not automatically make it Canonical/Approved, publish it orselect a Baseline |\n\n| Received transition | Typed meaning / decision boundary |\n|---|---|\n| Current Specification → Architectural Extension | Proposal derived from identified prior contract; not executed change orrequired consumer edge |\n| Architectural Extension → Compatibility Verification | Proposed/resulting exact artifact submitted to scoped compatibility evaluation; actual phase/executor/context recorded |\n| Compatibility Verification → Constraint Verification | Related evidence requirements, not a mandatory serial runtime order orsuccess inferred from earlier stage |\n| Constraint Verification → Canonical Specification | Evidence informs an independently applicable governed edition/decision; no automatic canonical status, approval, publication orBaseline inclusion |\n<!-- end-original-section-view -->\n\nFor an actual necessary normative oridentity/domain/path/representation transition:\n\n1. Preserve exact input artifacts/rules/editions, actual observations and failed checks. Identify the need, alternatives, rationale, prior/new governing rules, responsible authority, subjects/domains and scope. A finding, authorship orlocal PASS alone grants no allocation, mutation, ownership transfer orapproval.\n2. Assess affected semantic/record owners, consumers and indispensable consumer→provider interfaces, mandatory State/Canonical Reference/lifecycle/extension requirements and actual reciprocal/cycle claims. Reconcile conflicting governing authority before asserting integrated conformity; do not relabel away a real required edge.\n3. Record compatibility and exact old→new mappings, domain/canonical aliases, collisions/reservations/history, same-document continuity and intended reference/exact-edition/pinned-snapshot effects. Preserve allocated/retired non-reuse and immutable historical selections; no arbitrary key reassignment orsilent retargeting.\n4. Apply only the separately authorized scoped change with actual contributor/event provenance, appropriate working Version/Revision/history and coordinated transition evidence. Preserve earlier failed/pending events and original bytes; do not invent missing allocation, field, state orapproval facts.\n5. Validate the resulting exact artifacts/editions in the subsequent appropriate operation. Record actual rules, checks, executor, observations, coverage/limits and remaining owner/consumer work; later changed inputs require new assessment. Local PASS is not complete implementation rollout, architectural approval, publication orBaseline membership.\n\nFor this review, REVIEW identifies findings without source modification; UPDATE persists the justified successor and pending corrections; VALIDATE examines those exact bytes later. Retain the pause between operations. The view does not collapse these phases orsubstitute for actual approval/selection authority.\n\nDID-014§14's additive/backward-compatible/core-not-extensible restriction remains a real peer requirement pending responsible reconciliation with common assessed evolution. This protocol is not a silent waiver or an executed migration.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "10",
+          "text": "# 10. Version Compatibility\n\nThe five original version-compatibility subjects remain as scoped preservation requirements, not unconditional guarantees about all future implementations.\n\n| Original subject | Qualified compatibility / continuity obligation |\n|---|---|\n| preserve existing identities | Preserve assigned same-document qualified key, reservations/history and intended interpretation; no reassignment to another document |\n| preserve existing identifier semantics | Preserve responsible canonical/domain interpretation and traceable aliases; necessary governing-contract transition follows §9 |\n| preserve architectural contracts | Assess actual prior/new owner/provider/consumer/interfaces; ordinary extensions preserve meaning, necessary normative revision has explicit authority/impact/version/transition evidence |\n| preserve interoperability | Evaluate actual affected representations/adapters/consumers and observations; no completed global rollout inferred |\n| preserve canonical references | Preserve intended qualified document/exact-edition/context and immutable snapshot selection; actual connection validity/live retrieval needs observed evidence |\n\nExisting assignments and historical evidence are not silently invalidated orretargeted. Necessary incompatible governing-contract revision must be explicit, not called compatible merely because a Version changed. Actual mandatory peer authority and interfaces must be reconciled before a broader conformance claim; missing/conflicting evidence cannot be hidden by a successful subset.\n\nWorking-document editions follow Policy§5:compatible correction retains Version; compatible additive capability increments minor; incompatible contract change increments major and resets minor to0. Each persisted changed edition increments Revision monotonically across Version changes. Unchanged-source validation changes neither coordinate and is recorded centrally. These rules do not define every entity/domain allocation orlifecycle. Changed working successors are Draft/Baseline Not assigned absent exact inclusion evidence; received Canonical/Baseline1.0 is history, not inherited approval.\n\n---\n\n"
+        }
+      ],
