+      "literal_comparison_clauses": [
+        {
+          "section": "1",
+          "text": "# 1. Purpose\n\nThis document provides non-normative architectural recommendations for applying the Document Identity component under explicit subjects, domains and responsible owner contracts. It complements applicable Architecture, Model, Schema, Validation, Governance, Relationships, Constraints and Extensibility sources without replacing their authority.\n\nIts advice concerns applicable assigned normalized logical documents, their identity-related records and actual proposed operations. Distinguish pre-allocation subjects, physical files, exact editions/status, other entity keys, descriptors, registrations and graph projections. Every README.md, CHANGELOG.md and ArchitecturalMilestones.md remains non-normalized at every location; file or entity presence alone is not an allocation decision.\n\nRecommended practices support traceability, maintainability and compatible interpretation where assessed. They do not guarantee every implementation's interoperability or full consistency, create a runtime service or authorize allocation, migration, approval, publication or Baseline selection. Actual claims need identified rules, sources/editions, artifacts/context, checks, executor, observations, coverage and limits.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "2",
+          "text": "# 2. Introduction\n\nAlthough the recommendations presented here are not normative requirements, they can guide the application of independently applicable mandatory owner contracts. The advisory wording does not waive those contracts or introduce new universal mandatory rules. Type Reference identifies the document's consultation responsibility; explicit applicable declarations and references, not Type, title or directory, determine authority.\n\nRead each recommendation against its intended subject, responsible domain rules and exact source editions. Preserve unresolved mandatory State/Canonical Reference/lifecycle, extension and required-interface authority rather than inventing an accepted merged contract or selecting a conflicting rule to claim success.\n\nA successful local correction check is not full identity/component/framework conformity or formal architectural approval. Prior semantic or attribution-only results retain their exact examined bytes, editions and scope; later content needs its own assessment.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "4",
+          "text": "# 4. Identity Assignment\n\nThe three original assignment recommendations remain. Apply them to actual intended normalized document/domain subjects and responsible allocation decisions; these recommendations supply no generation algorithm or universal assignment time.\n\n---\n\n## 4.1. Assign Identity Once\n\nRecommend one governing assignment for the applicable logical document under its domain contract, with actual allocation/association authority and collision/reservation/history evidence. Architectural recognition, a file's creation, registration or a generated header is not by itself assignment.\n\nOrdinary content, metadata, classification, location, format and edition/status evolution should preserve the assigned key. Copies and exact editions of the same document do not automatically need new identities. A genuinely different logical document needs its own applicable subject/allocation assessment; necessary governing-contract transitions follow §5.2 rather than being disguised as ordinary evolution.\n\n---\n\n## 4.2. Assign Identity Early\n\nRecommend establishing an applicable document's identity early enough to support its actual traceability needs, when the responsible domain and allocation decision/evidence are available. Governance entry alone neither allocates a key nor proves authority, uniqueness or which lifecycle assertion applies.\n\nPre-allocation subjects and excluded functional files remain distinct. Do not invent an identity or reservation simply to satisfy an early-assignment recommendation, and do not infer an organizational role or permission from authorship, file custody or architectural naming.\n\n---\n\n## 4.3. Avoid Temporary Identities\n\nAvoid presenting temporary tracking labels as canonical assigned document identities. Where provisional tracking is necessary, distinguish its subject, scope and responsible record from the governing Domain+Identifier key; no new universal lifecycle state or Metadata Element is defined here.\n\nCanonical assignment still needs the actual responsible domain/series decision and collision/reservation/history evidence. Preserve traceable provisional-to-assigned interpretation, original records and affected reference/context mappings without silently promoting a placeholder, fabricating a past allocation or retargeting historical evidence. A matching string alone is not that decision.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "5",
+          "text": "# 5. Identity Preservation\n\nThe three original preservation subjects remain. Recommend preserving assigned same-document identity while separately assessing actual record, representation, reference, consumer and decision effects.\n\n---\n\n## 5.1. Preserve Identity During Revisions\n\nContent revisions, corrections, restructurings, metadata and classification changes should preserve the assigned qualified key under its governing contract. Supporting state/reference/descriptor/registration/projection records and documentary Version/Revision/Status can change under their own responsible rules.\n\nKeep exact changed artifacts, contributors, applicable source editions and observations traceable. Preserving logical identity does not automatically validate changed bytes, merge conflicting mandatory records or extend an earlier validation result to a successor.\n\n---\n\n## 5.2. Preserve Identity During Migration\n\nRepository, storage and technology movement should preserve the assigned logical key through ordinary migration. Actual locators, indexes, aliases, freshness, interfaces and references can be affected; examine prior/new representations and intended targets rather than assuming the movement is harmless.\n\nBefore a necessary normative/domain/path/representation transition, recommend identifying prior/new responsible rules and actual authority, rationale/alternatives, exact affected semantic/record owners, consumers and indispensable interfaces. Assess compatibility and document exact mappings/qualification, canonical aliases, collisions/reservations/history, same-document continuity and reference/exact-edition/pinned-snapshot effects. Preserve allocated/retired non-reuse and never silently reassign a key to another document or retarget an immutable historical member.\n\nApply only the separately authorized scoped change with actual contribution, edition/history and transition evidence; retain original failed checks and later validate the resulting exact artifacts. Advice or a local PASS alone grants no migration or mutation. No migration is executed by this document.\n\n---\n\n## 5.3. Preserve Identity During Publication\n\nDifferent published formats or physical copies of the same logical document should preserve its governing identity rather than automatically allocate additional document keys. Exact editions, representations and publication records remain distinguishable with their source/context evidence.\n\nPublication, formal approval and Baseline membership are separate decisions. A Draft successor does not inherit received Canonical naming, approval or selection into Baseline1.0. Preserve immutable historical exact-edition selections and relevant pinned relation context; a current path or stable identity alone does not identify an exact approved member.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "6",
+          "text": "# 6. Identifier Management\n\nThe three original identifier-management subjects remain. Recommend responsible qualified interpretation and stable assigned allocation, not a context-free string convention or running resolver.\n\n---\n\n## 6.1. Prefer Stable Identifiers\n\nPreserve the assigned canonical identifier interpretation and same-document association through ordinary evolution under its governing Domain+Identifier contract. Distinguish that key from a canonical rendering, location, alias, descriptor or exact edition.\n\nFor this corpus the domain is Documentation Framework, represented by DF. DF-SSS-NNN uses a registered uppercase three-letter stable allocation series and positive three-digit001–999 allocation. 000 is not allocated; allocated/retired numbers remain reserved, and gaps prove no availability. At999, register a traceable unused continuation series rather than widen syntax or recycle. Current Family/Type/Component/path/status/edition does not reconstruct an assigned key. Other domains need their own explicit contracts.\n\nActual uniqueness needs responsible canonicalization, assignments, population/collision/reservation/history and coverage evidence. Syntax or a partial index is not proof of global uniqueness or availability. Necessary governing-contract transitions use §5.2; this source allocates or renumbers no key.\n\n---\n\n## 6.2. Avoid Semantic Encoding\n\nAvoid deriving a governing key from values expected to change. The five original examples remain:\n\n- repository names;\n- storage locations;\n- workflow states;\n- file paths;\n- implementation technologies.\n\nCurrent mutable Family/Type/Component and metadata values likewise should not regenerate an assigned key. A registered stable series may retain a recorded historical origin or purpose without encoding the document's current classification. Do not renumber an existing valid identifier merely because that historical series code is meaningful. Domain prefixes and legitimate canonical representations remain governed by their responsible contracts, not rejected merely as semantic encoding.\n\n---\n\n## 6.3. Maintain Identifier Consistency\n\nRecommend consistent intended qualified document identity and canonical interpretation across document-targeting references. Exact-edition references additionally need the responsible coordinates/bytes and context. Other endpoint kinds follow their own typed identity contracts; not every architectural reference targets a document.\n\nLegitimate aliases, alternative representations and adapters need logical/domain mappings, responsible source/edition provenance and actual interface compatibility; they are not solely implementation design choices. Repeated renderings of one intended key are not alone competing allocations.\n\nIncoming references may be0..N; optional incoming population does not waive validity of each actual reference. Record actual alias/target interpretation and dangling, ambiguous, stale or unavailable observations. Historical interpretation is distinct from current connection validity and live retrieval. Never silently create a key or retarget an immutable historical member to make a reference appear valid; no perpetual resolver is promised.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "7",
+          "text": "# 7. Cross-Component Integration\n\nThe five original integration subjects remain. Distinguish identity semantic ownership under common Architecture/Policy from actual descriptor/category/connection/registration/projection owners and other entity domains. Documentary authority, required consumption, projection, navigation and execution have different meanings.\n\n---\n\n## 7.1. Metadata\n\nMetadata should associate descriptors with the intended applicable document or exact edition/context without redefining document-key meaning. Its Dictionary/Schema/Profile/Instance and other subject/entity contracts remain separate. Traceable representation of identity values, with responsible source/edition provenance, is not itself prohibited duplication or a competing allocation.\n\nMetadata→Document Identity is the actual required identification claim for the applicable normalized document described. The functional DID README also states: “The Document Identity component depends on the Metadata component for the canonical definition of metadata concepts used to describe document identity.” Current DID-009§5.1.1/§10 retains both claims. Recommend keeping these actual reciprocal requirements explicit pending responsible core-versus-descriptor/interface/source-edition reconciliation; projection or navigation relabelling does not dissolve a genuine required cycle.\n\nNo absent Metadata Element/Profile, mandatory state or approval fact is invented by representing identity information.\n\n---\n\n## 7.2. Classification\n\nClassification should categorize the intended document under its own category/assignment contracts and consume the applicable document-key meaning without redefining it. Category/definition/entity keys are not automatically normalized document IDs. Changes to current classification labels do not reconstruct an assigned stable document series/key.\n\n---\n\n## 7.3. Registry\n\nRegistry entries should identify their intended qualified document or exact edition/context with source provenance and freshness limits. Registration/index records retain Registry ownership; internal entry keys and locators do not replace canonical document identity.\n\nRepeated entries or copied key values are not alone duplicate allocations. Registration is distinct from allocation, approval and completeness of population/history checks. No mandatory universal Registry intermediary or running lookup service is implied.\n\n---\n\n## 7.4. Relationships\n\nRelationships should preserve typed connection/endpoints and provenance under its owner contracts. Document endpoints use the intended qualified document/exact-edition context in §6.3; other endpoint types keep their own identity rules.\n\nAn actual connection still needs its applicable type/direction/subject/context and observed validity; canonical target identity alone does not establish the whole connection's correctness or permanent retrieval. Connection changes do not allocate identity, authorize silent target replacement or redefine the document key.\n\n---\n\n## 7.5. Knowledge Graph\n\nDocument nodes should preserve intended qualified document-key meaning while Knowledge Graph owns logical node/edge projection and mutable properties. Source identity and Relationships connection records remain authoritative with actual edition/context provenance and refresh limits.\n\nKnowledge Graph→Document Identity for document nodes and Knowledge Graph→Relationships for typed connections are selected required contracts, not removed because data is projected. Logical representation/interface requirements are architectural responsibilities; storage/runtime choices remain implementation concerns. Derived records do not establish competing authority, new allocation, synchronized consumers or complete graph/acyclicity proof.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "8",
+          "text": "# 8. Governance Recommendations\n\nRecommend preserving assigned same-document key through ordinary document operations while lifecycle/state/edition/supporting records follow their responsible contracts. Governance is the area grouping Workflow, Policies, Reviews and Baselines, not one operational engine. Applicable DMS, tool or manual processes execute actual operations, with CMS governing changes in its scope.\n\nApproval workflows, reviews, publication, archival and repository movement need actual responsible rules/editions, authority/decision, exact subject/context, executor and observations. Authorship, custody, received Canonical status or a local PASS alone grants no operational permission. Document Status, file/edition/index deletion or archiving does not automatically allocate/retire the logical identity or grant an unlisted DID-006 transition.\n\nBaselines needs exact eligible approved editions, actual selection and relevant pinned relationship context or traceable verification evidence. Logical identity, publication copies and validation results alone select no member. Draft successors do not inherit approval/membership or retarget immutable history. Identity lifecycle, document edition/Status, review, validation, approval, publication and Baseline inclusion remain distinct.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "9",
+          "text": "# 9. Validation Recommendations\n\nThe five original recommended validation occasions remain advisory prompts to identify applicable checks, not a universal serial workflow or permission to act:\n\n- when assigning a new identity;\n- before document registration;\n- before publication;\n- during repository migration;\n- during consistency verification.\n\nFor each actual occasion identify the intended claim/domain/subject, exact input artifacts/editions/bytes/context, responsible mandatory rules/source editions, executor/method, performed/unperformed checks, observations, coverage and limits. Assignment checks need actual domain/series/reservation/history evidence; registration/publication/migration/consistency occasions have their own affected target/representation/authority scopes. Advice to check is not proof the checks ran or every mandatory requirement was covered.\n\nDID-007 distinguishes Valid, Invalid, Inconsistent and Unresolvable observations. A successful subset is narrower evidence, not full identity/component/framework validity. Missing/unexecuted/conflicting mandatory evidence withholds broader success without automatically inventing Invalid or Unresolvable. Preserve actual supported negative observations and exact rules/context rather than hiding them with a silent precedence rule.\n\nMandatory State/Canonical Reference and complete-record/lifecycle authority in DID-005/006/014 remain pending owner work. This recommendation neither waives fields nor invents an accepted merged schema. Evaluate unambiguous subsets with limits; reconcile governing authority before broader conformity.\n\nEvaluation is separate from writing a result record and from authorized correction/allocation. Recommend preserving original observations/failed checks, obtaining actual responsible authority for a scoped change, recording owner/consumer impact, contributor/version/history and later exact validation as in §5.2. A result does not approve/publish a successor or select Baseline membership; changed inputs/rules/context need their own assessment.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "10",
+          "text": "# 10. Extensibility Recommendations\n\nThe four original extension recommendation subjects remain for ordinary compatible additions:\n\n- preserve backward compatibility;\n- preserve canonical identity semantics;\n- preserve interoperability;\n- remain additive rather than destructive.\n\nPrefer preserving compatible responsible meanings and consumer contracts where feasible. Do not treat an extension label as proof of compatibility or permission to silently redefine a canonical contract. Necessary assessed normative/foundational revision follows common Architecture AP-015–018 and current DID-011§9: actual prior/new authority, rationale/alternatives, exact affected owners/consumers/indispensable interfaces, compatibility/version, mappings/reservations/history/reference/snapshot continuity and transition evidence before separately authorized change.\n\nDID-014§14 still requires additive/backward-compatible extensions and states that core identity semantics are not extensible. Preserve that actual restrictive peer claim and pending authority reconciliation with the controlled-evolution approach; advisory recommendations do not waive owner contracts, claim universal agreement or authorize migration. Mandatory field/lifecycle and actual reciprocal required-interface work remains visible. No consumer rollout or implemented interoperability is inferred.\n\nWorking-document edition changes follow Policy§5: compatible correction retains Version; compatible additive capability increments minor; incompatible contract/convention change increments major and resets minor to0. Each persisted changed edition increments Revision monotonically across Versions. Unchanged-source validation changes neither coordinate and is tracked centrally. This document's advisory nature remains unchanged; those rules do not define every entity/domain allocation or lifecycle.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "13",
+          "text": "# 13. Relationship with Other Specifications\n\nAll eleven original specification contribution subjects remain, with their intended declared identity and qualified consultation role. These are selected responsible source uses, not a claim that every peer is approved or globally reconciled.\n\n| Specification | Intended source | Qualified contribution |\n|---|---|---|\n| Document Identity Architecture | [DF-DID-001](../01-Architecture/DF-DID-001-DocumentIdentityArchitecture.md) | Applicable scope, semantic ownership and actual consumer boundaries |\n| Document Identity Concepts | [DF-DID-002](../01-Architecture/DF-DID-002%20-%20Document%20Identity%20Concepts.md) | Logical document/key/domain/reference and evolution vocabulary |\n| Document Identity Model | [DF-DID-003](../01-Architecture/DF-DID-003%20-%20Document%20Identity%20Model.md) | Qualified subjects, entity associations and reference cardinality |\n| Document Identity Elements | [DF-DID-004](../01-Architecture/DF-DID-004%20-%20Document%20Identity%20Elements.md) | Key versus supporting state/reference/record responsibility |\n| Document Identity Schema | [DF-DID-005](../02-Structure/DF-DID-005%20-%20Document%20Identity%20Schema.md) | Qualified corpus representation, selected schema and mandatory peer authority |\n| Document Identity Lifecycle | [DF-DID-006](../03-Behavior/DF-DID-006%20-%20Document%20Identity%20Lifecycle.md) | Pre-allocation/assignment/preservation/retirement assertions and selected transition/decision scope |\n| Document Identity Validation | [DF-DID-007](../03-Behavior/DF-DID-007%20-%20Document%20Identity%20Validation.md) | Actual responsible checks, outcome/coverage and nonmutating evaluation |\n| Document Identity Governance | [DF-DID-008](../03-Behavior/DF-DID-008%20-%20Document%20Identity%20Governance.md) | Semantic/operational boundaries, evidence and separately authorized change |\n| Document Identity Relationships | [DF-DID-009](../02-Structure/DF-DID-009%20-%20Document%20Identity%20Relationships.md) | Typed actual consumer/provider, record, reference and executor contracts |\n| Document Identity Constraints | [DF-DID-010](../02-Structure/DF-DID-010%20-%20Document%20Identity%20Constraints.md) | Scoped mandatory constraints and actual compliance/authority limits |\n| Document Identity Extensibility | [DF-DID-011](../03-Behavior/DF-DID-011%20-%20Document%20Identity%20Extensibility.md) | Ordinary compatible extensions versus necessary assessed normative revision |\n\nThis document remains explicitly non-normative. Mandatory authority comes from an actual applicable owner declaration/contract and governing context, not a title, directory or Type value. Compare exact source editions/status/Baseline and rule scopes; a linked Draft source or received Canonical label is not formal approval. Mandatory State/Canonical Reference/lifecycle, restrictive extension and reciprocal required-interface claims remain explicit pending responsible reconciliation.\n\n---\n\n"
+        }
