+          "section": "1",
+          "text": "# 1. Purpose\n\nThis dictionary defines component-normative vocabulary for Document Identity within explicitly qualified subjects, domains and responsible contracts. The definitions contained herein are normative for the Document Identity component in that declared scope; this is not a claim of universal authority over every framework domain or of an approved implementation.\n\nCommon Architecture/Policy and responsible identity, descriptor, category, connection, registration, projection and lifecycle sources retain their applicable authority. DF-ARC-006 supplies owner-linked common consultation vocabulary and contextual aliases rather than replacing every detailed domain contract. Explicit applicable declarations, not Type Reference, a title or a repeated definition, establish normative authority.\n\nThe dictionary covers applicable assigned normalized logical documents and their identity-related meanings, while distinguishing pre-allocation subjects, files, exact editions/Status, mutable records and other entity domains. Every README.md, CHANGELOG.md and ArchitecturalMilestones.md remains non-normalized at every location. No identity, Metadata field/Profile, permission or operational service is created by naming a term.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "2",
+          "text": "# 2. Introduction\n\nInterpret terms with their stated subject/domain/context, responsible source editions and controlled alias limits. Generic words such as Identity, Identifier, Lifecycle and Validation also have other entity-domain uses; documentary context does not erase those contracts.\n\nWhere common terminology and a component source appear to conflict, identify the actual responsible meaning, previous/current editions and affected consumers under common governing contracts. Do not declare either glossary universally superior, silently select one claim or infer that all sources have been reconciled. Preserve unresolved authority and narrower unambiguous observations; coordinated owner decisions and propagation remain necessary before broader conformity claims.\n\nCanonical semantic meaning is distinct from document lifecycle Status, formal approval, publication or exact Baseline inclusion. A dictionary's presence, a working Draft source or a local correction PASS grants none of those outcomes. Prior semantic/attribution-only results retain their exact bytes, editions and scope.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "3.6",
+          "text": "## 3.6. Document Identity Lifecycle\n\n**Definition**\n\nThe subject-specific identity assertion/evolution contract covering an applicable documentary pre-allocation subject and, after assignment, continuing same-document preservation and actual retirement of active use within a declared scope.\n\n**Notes**\n\nDID-006 distinguishes Unassigned pre-allocation from Assigned, Preserved and Retired assertions; a nonexistent assigned key is not presumed to occupy Unassigned. Its eight selected transition rows require actual events, responsible decision/rules and evidence, not a universal permission graph. Identity Lifecycle (§3.15) is a documentary-context alias with the same scope.\n\nDocument-edition Status, identity-state records, maturity, approval, publication and Baseline membership differ. Status, archiving or deletion of a file/edition/index alone does not retire the logical identity or grant an unlisted transition. Actual State/Canonical Reference and complete-record/lifecycle authority in DID-005/006/014 remains pending reconciliation, not waived or merged here.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "3.7",
+          "text": "## 3.7. Document Identity Model\n\n**Definition**\n\nThe responsible conceptual contract or selected view of documentary identity subjects, elements, associations and ownership boundaries in its explicitly declared scope.\n\n**Notes**\n\nA conceptual model is not automatically a complete accepted implementation record or object layout. DID-003 and detailed owners retain entity/cardinality meaning; DID-005 selected key/association view differs from actual mandatory State/Canonical Reference/lifecycle claims in DID-014. Preserve those requirements and pending authority rather than invent a reconciled merged model or infer completeness from a picture or glossary.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "3.8",
+          "text": "## 3.8. Document Identity Schema\n\n**Definition**\n\nThe responsible structural contract for the declared identity/record/association subjects, representation constraints and applicable cardinalities, as qualified by its source editions and owner rules.\n\n**Notes**\n\nDID-005 is the schema source; its selected assigned key/association scope does not remove DID-014 mandatory State/Canonical Reference or settle lifecycle authority. Canonical structure is not an approved universal record merely because a definition calls it canonical. Necessary schema/domain/representation evolution requires §9 prior/new authority, owner/consumer compatibility and transition evidence; no field, Metadata Element/Profile or accepted complete schema is fabricated.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "3.13",
+          "text": "## 3.13. Identity Governance\n\n**Definition**\n\nThe identity-domain requirements and responsibility/evidence boundaries governing assigned same-document key integrity, stability, preservation, reservations and responsible change under applicable contracts.\n\n**Notes**\n\nDID-008 owns detailed identity-governance requirements; common architecture and other semantic/record owners remain applicable. Governance is the area grouping Workflow, Policies, Reviews and Baselines documentary contracts. Actual DMS/tool/manual execution and CMS change scope are distinct and need real authority/decision/executor evidence. Documentary ownership, authorship or a local PASS is not operational permission, formal approval, publication or exact Baseline selection.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "3.14",
+          "text": "## 3.14. Identity Integrity\n\n**Definition**\n\nA scoped requirement or assessed property that the intended document-key association and applicable supporting facts satisfy their responsible completeness, provenance/authenticity and consistency rules in an identified subject/context.\n\n**Notes**\n\nThe received complete/authentic/internally-consistent qualities require actual applicable rules/editions, artifacts, authority, observations, coverage and limits; they are not an unconditional implemented guarantee. No universal authentication, cryptographic assurance or security mechanism is supplied by this definition.\n\nCompleteness depends on actual mandatory owner fields/records, including unresolved State/Canonical Reference/lifecycle authority. Missing evidence or conflicting authority withholds broader conformity without inventing a violation or an accepted merged schema. Integrity is distinct from document Status, approval, publication and Baseline membership.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "3.20",
+          "text": "## 3.20. Identity Validation\n\n**Definition**\n\nThe scoped evaluation of applicable responsible identity requirements against exact identified subjects, artifacts/editions and context, with performed checks, observations, coverage and limits.\n\n**Notes**\n\nDID-007 defines Valid, Invalid, Inconsistent and Unresolvable observations. An arbitrary successful subset is narrower evidence, not full identity/component/framework Valid; missing/unperformed/conflicting mandatory evidence withholds broader success without automatic fabricated failure. Preserve actual negative observations and unresolved authority.\n\nChecking is distinct from writing a result record and from separately authorized correction/allocation. A local result does not mutate the assessed key, grant permission, formally approve/publish a document or select an exact Baseline member. Validation (§3.29) is a contextual short form within this component, not ownership of every framework check.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "3.26",
+          "text": "## 3.26. Retirement\n\n**Definition**\n\nIn state-oriented use within the selected DID-006 contract, the Retired assertion following an actual responsible retirement event: active use ends in its declared scope while the same-document historical association and key reservation remain.\n\n**Notes**\n\nDistinguish this assertion from Identity Retirement event/decision (§3.18). DID-006 has no outgoing edge from Retired in its selected eight-transition scope; this does not establish every external lifecycle contract or a universal permission graph. Document archiving, obsolescence or file/edition/index deletion is not automatically retirement; history and reservations remain even if current content is unavailable. The received no-longer-active-document phrase does not erase original document association or permit reuse.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
